    public static final int WHIRLWIND = 43;
    /** A blizzard closed around the two of them, and no way out of it. */
    public static final int SNOWSTORM = 44;

    // ---- Phase three: the winter fights for him ----
    /** Wards he cannot be touched through until they are broken. */
    public static final int PILLAR_WARDS = 45;
    /** A ring of orbs, thrown one at a time. */
    public static final int ORB_BARRAGE = 46;
    /** A marked circle, then a column of cold through it. */
    public static final int ENERGY_PILLAR = 47;

    /** Inside his own blizzard: a run of stooping dives, one after another. */
    public static final int BERSERK_DIVE = 48;

    /** Shot in the chest by nothing at all: it opens, and the winter comes out. */
    public static final int HEART_BARRAGE = 49;
    /** A cloud held over their head, raining spears that stand where they land. */
    public static final int SPEAR_CLOUD = 50;

    /** A boot in the chest: no sword in it, and it exists to make room. */
    public static final int KICK_SHOVE = 51;
    /** A chain of ice thrown, hauled in, and answered with a half-turn cut. */
    public static final int CHAIN_PULL = 52;

    /** One past the highest attack state, so the cooldown table covers them all. */
    /**
     * The executioner. He pins the target with Slowness III, hangs a
     * house-sized greatsword over their head, and drops it. Phase three only.
     */
    public static final int DOOM_BLADE = 53;

    /** The refusal: shield up, nothing lands, and greed is answered. */
    public static final int SHIELD_WALL = 54;

    /** Two raps of the blade on the shield rim. Costs nothing, says plenty. */
    public static final int GUARD_TAP = 55;

    /** Three hits into the front of the guard, and he answers with both. */
    public static final int SHIELD_PUNISH = 56;

    /**
     * Blade up from the hip, then the shield into whatever it lifted.
     *
     * <p>The first phase had no opener that MOVED the player - everything
     * either held ground or punished them for touching the guard. This takes
     * them off their feet for half a second and then hits them while they are
     * still in the air, which is the one thing a shield-and-sword stance
     * should be able to do and could not.
     */
    public static final int RISING_CUT = 57;

    /**
     * The lower rim of the shield tapped on the floor, and a ring that widens.
     *
     * <p>Deliberately NOT the stomp. The stomp is spikes coming up under you
     * and it throws; this is a wall of air travelling out, and it shoves. Low
     * damage, long reach, and it grows as it goes - a zoning tool that makes
     * standing at mid range cost something, which is exactly the distance the
     * first phase was otherwise comfortable to sit at.
     */
    public static final int SHIELD_PULSE = 58;

    /**
     * One boot down, and the floor tears away from him in a LANE.
     *
     * <p>Not the ground stomp. That one breaks in rings all the way round,
     * which is an attack you answer by backing off in any direction at all.
     * This one picks the line to the player at the moment the heel lands and
     * runs down it, so the answer is to move SIDEWAYS - a different read, a
     * different tell, and the one Cataclysm's bosses build their floor work
     * around.
     *
     * <p>The blocks come from floorBurst, which already launches them
     * straight up off the block grid rather than spraying them: a grid of
     * cubes rising together reads as FLOOR being hit, scattered ones read as
     * an explosion. Stepping that burst outward two ticks at a time is what
     * turns it into something travelling.
     */
    public static final int RIFT_STOMP = 59;

    /**
     * THE RITE. The blade goes into the floor and is DRAGGED, and what climbs
     * out of the cut is Hrimthar.
     *
     * <p>It replaces a bare spawn call. A miniboss that simply appears is an
     * event that happened to the player; one that is visibly torn out of the
     * ground by the boss they are already fighting is something the boss DID,
     * and the whole difference is in whether there is a shot of him doing the
     * work. Five seconds of it: he comes down out of the air, buries the
     * sword to the hilt, walks it six blocks through the floor, and wrenches
     * it out.
     */
    public static final int GOLEM_RITE = 60;
    /** Off: the Monstrosity is the Ice Prison's guardian, not his summon. */
    private static final boolean GOLEM_RITE_ENABLED = false;

    /**
     * One past the highest attack id, and it is load-bearing.
     *
     * <p>{@code attackCooldowns} is sized from this, and {@code ready(state)}
     * returns false for anything outside the array - silently. So every attack
     * added past the old value of 61 was offered, refused, and never once
     * chosen: the skyhook, and all three of the combo pieces. They were not
     * failing to load or failing to animate, they were never legal.
     *
     * <p>The lesson is that a bounds check written as a guard became a filter.
     * ready() returning false for an out-of-range id looks defensive and is
     * indistinguishable from "on cooldown", which is why nothing anywhere
     * complained. Any new attack id must move this number.
     */
    // 104, because SPIRIT_WARDS is 103 and this array is indexed BY state id:
    // the highest id in the roster plus one, always. RIP_RISE is 101 and
    // TWIN_RUSH is 102; SPIRIT_WARDS took the next number and this count went
    // up with it in the same edit, which is the only way this stays true.
    //
    // The warning above was written after the last time this bit, and it bit
    // again the very next time an id was added: a new attack numbered past the
    // end throws the moment anything writes its cooldown. Crash of
    // 2026-09-24 09:08, index 101 into length 78.
